B.Us=new A.eY("Morning Routine","I wake up early every morning and drink a glass of water. Then I go for a short walk before breakfast. This simple routine keeps me fresh and ready for the day. I open the window first and let the cool morning air come into the room. The light is soft and the street outside is still quiet. I wash my face with cold water and feel awake at once. I brush my teeth slowly and comb my hair in front of the mirror. I put on clean clothes and make my bed before I leave the room. A tidy room helps me feel calm and ready to work. I walk to the small park near my house and breathe in the fresh air. The trees are green and the birds sing in the branches above me. I see a few old men walking slowly on the path. I see children going to school with bags on their backs. I walk for about twenty minutes and then I turn back toward home. My legs feel light and my mind feels clear after the walk. At home I sit at the table and eat a simple breakfast. I eat bread with eggs and drink a cup of hot milk. Sometimes I eat fruit with my breakfast when fruit is in season. I eat slowly and I do not rush through my food. After breakfast I wash the dishes and put them back in their place. I check my bag and make sure I have my books and my keys. I write down the three most important things I must do today. A short list helps me remember what matters most. I start with the hardest task while my mind is still fresh. I work for some time and then I take a short break. During the break I drink water and stretch my arms and legs. I do not look at my phone during the first hour of the day. This helps me stay focused on my work. In the afternoon I take a short rest when I feel tired. A short rest gives me new energy for the rest of the day. I eat lunch at home with my family when I can. We talk about our day and share simple news with each other. In the evening I go for another short walk if the weather is good. The evening air is cool and the sky turns soft and gold. I come home and read a few pages of a book before dinner. Reading helps me learn new words and new ideas. After dinner I help clean the table and wash the dishes. I plan the next day for a few minutes before I sleep. I keep my clothes and my bag ready for the morning. I go to bed at the same time every night. A fixed bedtime helps me wake up early without an alarm. Good sleep makes the whole next day feel easy and bright. A simple morning routine may look small but it shapes the whole day. Small good habits done every day bring steady change in life.",B.bp)
B.Ur=new A.eY("A Walk in the Park","The park near my house is small but very peaceful. Children play on the swings while their parents sit on the benches and talk. Birds sing in the trees above. I go to the park almost every day in the evening. The gate is open and the path inside is clean and wide. Green grass covers the ground on both sides of the path. Tall trees give shade and keep the air cool and fresh. Flowers grow near the fence in bright red and yellow colours. Their smell is sweet and light in the evening breeze. I walk slowly around the park and enjoy the quiet. A dog runs after a ball and barks with joy. Its owner calls its name and laughs at its happy run. Two boys play football on the open grass in the middle. They run fast and kick the ball to each other with care. A girl rides her small bicycle around the path with a smile. Her father walks behind her and watches her with love. Old men sit under a big tree and talk about old days. They drink tea from small cups and smile at each other. Women walk in small groups and talk about home and family. Their voices are soft and full of warmth. A man reads a newspaper on a bench near the pond. Ducks swim slowly in the pond and make small rings in the water. The water is clear and the sun shines on its face. Butterflies fly over the flowers and rest on soft petals. Their wings are bright and move gently in the light. I sit on a bench for a while and watch the clouds in the sky. The clouds move slowly and change their shape as they drift. The sun goes down and the sky turns pink and gold. Lights come on along the path and the park looks warm and safe. People start to go home one by one as the dark grows. The park becomes quiet again and the birds go to their nests. I feel calm and happy after my walk in the park. My body feels light and my mind feels free from worry. A walk in the park is a simple joy that costs nothing at all. It gives health to the body and peace to the mind. I hope the park stays clean and green for many years. Every town should have a small park like this one. Children need open space to play and run and grow. Old people need a quiet place to sit and rest and talk. A park brings neighbours together and builds a kind community. I will keep coming here every day for my evening walk. I bring a small bottle of water and drink it slowly as I walk. The fresh air fills my lungs and makes me feel strong and alive. I greet the same friendly faces each day and we share a short hello. These small greetings make the park feel like a second home to me. The park near my house will always be my favourite place.",B.bp)
B.Ut=new A.eY("Making Tea","First you boil the water in a small pot. Then you add the tea leaves and let them sit for a few minutes. Finally you pour the tea into a cup and add milk if you like. Making tea is a simple skill that every home needs. You need only a pot, water, tea leaves, milk, and sugar. You also need a cup and a small spoon for stirring. Start by filling the pot with clean fresh water. Put the pot on the stove and turn the flame to medium heat. Wait for the water to boil and make small bubbles. Do not leave the pot alone while the water heats. When the water boils, add one spoon of tea leaves for each cup. Let the leaves sit in the hot water for three or four minutes. The water will turn a deep brown colour as the tea grows strong. You can add a small piece of ginger for a fresh taste. Some people add a few green cardamom pods for a sweet smell. Stir the tea slowly and let the leaves settle at the bottom. Now add warm milk to the pot and stir it well. Add sugar to taste and stir again until it melts. Turn off the flame and let the tea rest for a minute. Pour the tea through a small strainer into a clean cup. The strainer keeps the leaves out of the cup. Hold the cup with care because the tea is very hot. Take a small sip first to test the heat and the taste. Good tea is warm, smooth, and full of gentle flavour. If the tea is too strong, add a little more milk. If the tea is too sweet, use less sugar next time. Serve the tea with biscuits or a small piece of cake. Tea tastes best when you drink it with family and friends. In the morning, tea helps you wake up and feel fresh. In the evening, tea helps you relax after a long day. In winter, a hot cup of tea warms your hands and your heart. In summer, you can let the tea cool and add ice to it. Clean the pot and the cups after you finish your tea. Wash them with warm water and put them back in their place. A clean kitchen makes the next cup of tea taste even better. Learn to make tea well and your guests will feel at home. A cup of tea can start a good talk and build a warm friendship. Tea is more than a drink in many homes around the world. Tea has travelled across many lands and every land has its own way of making it. Some people like strong black tea while others like light green tea with lemon. In some homes, tea is served in small glasses, and in others it is served in large mugs. The best tea is made with care, patience, and clean water. Take time to enjoy each cup and share it with those you love. It is a sign of care, welcome, and simple joy in daily life.",B.bp)
B.dkD=s([B.Us,B.Ur,B.Ut],t.LJ)
B.dkT=s([35,30,20,25,30,35,30,25,25],t.n)
B.Up=new A.eY("The Value of Practice","Learning any new skill requires consistent practice rather than occasional bursts of effort. A person who studies for twenty minutes every single day will usually progress faster than someone who studies for three hours only once a week. Small, steady habits build lasting ability. Practice works because the brain learns through repetition and rest. Each short session adds a small layer of skill on top of the last one. The gaps between sessions give the mind time to settle what it has learned. Long cramming sessions often feel productive in the moment but fade quickly. A learner who returns to the same skill day after day builds a firmer base. The key is to show up even on days when energy and mood are low. A short session on a tired day still keeps the habit alive. Missing one day is not a failure, but missing many days in a row breaks the rhythm. The best learners treat practice as a daily appointment that they keep. They choose a fixed time and a quiet place and guard that time well. They also set a clear and small goal for each session. A clear goal turns vague effort into focused work. Feedback matters as much as repetition in good practice. A learner who checks mistakes and corrects them improves far faster than one who repeats the same errors. Slow and careful practice builds correct habits from the start. Speed should come later, after accuracy has become natural. Many beginners rush toward speed and then struggle to fix bad habits. Patient practice avoids that trap and saves time in the long run. Variety also helps practice stay fresh and useful. A typist can mix speed drills with accuracy drills and real passages. A language learner can mix reading, listening, speaking, and writing. Each kind of practice strengthens the skill from a different side. Rest is part of practice, not a break from it. Sleep helps the brain store new patterns learned during the day. A tired learner gains little from forcing another long session. Short breaks inside a session also keep focus sharp. Progress in any skill is rarely a straight line upward. Some weeks bring fast gains and some weeks feel flat and slow. Plateaus are normal and they often come just before a new jump in skill. Learners who expect this pattern stay calm and keep going. Those who expect constant fast progress often quit too soon. Tracking small wins helps motivation stay strong through slow weeks. A simple notebook or a chart can show how far a learner has come. Looking back at early work often reveals growth that daily effort hides. Practice also builds confidence in a quiet and steady way. Each finished session is proof that the learner can do hard things. Over months, that proof adds up into real self trust. The value of practice is not only in the skill itself. It teaches patience, discipline, and honest self knowledge. Those qualities help in study, in work, and in daily life. Start small, stay regular, correct mistakes kindly, and keep going.",B.d6)
B.Uu=new A.eY("Cities at Night","When the sun sets, a city changes completely. Office buildings that were full of noise and movement fall quiet, while restaurants and markets nearby begin to fill with people. The streets take on a different rhythm, calmer in some corners and livelier in others. Lights come on one by one across the skyline as darkness grows. Windows glow warm and yellow in homes high above the street. Shop signs shine in bright colours and call out to people passing by. Street lamps cast pools of light on the pavements below. The city that felt hard and busy by day now feels softer and more human. Workers leave their offices and hurry toward buses and trains. Some stop for a quick meal at a small food stall on the corner. The smell of fresh bread and grilled food drifts through the cool air. Friends meet outside cafes and greet each other with smiles. They sit at small tables and talk about their day over hot drinks. Families walk together in shopping streets looking for clothes and gifts. Children point at bright toys in shop windows with wide eyes. In one part of the city, a night market opens with rows of stalls. Vendors call out prices and show their goods to curious buyers. Clothes, books, fruit, and handmade crafts fill the narrow lanes. Bargaining is part of the fun and both sides enjoy the game. In another part, the streets grow quiet and almost empty. A lone street sweeper pushes a cart along the kerb. A guard stands outside a closed bank and watches the road. Cats slip between parked cars and search for food in silence. The night city holds many different lives side by side. Nurses and doctors start their night shifts in hospitals. Bakers begin work while most people sleep, so bread is fresh by morning. Drivers carry goods through the city on quiet roads. Their work keeps the city running while others rest. Late at night, the sounds change and grow softer. Traffic thins and the hum of engines fades away. Footsteps echo on empty pavements and doors close with a soft sound. The moon rises above the rooftops and lends a silver light. Stars appear in patches of sky between tall buildings. For some people, night is the best time to think and create. Writers, artists, and students often work best when the city is still. The quiet gives their minds room to wander and make new links. Cities at night also raise questions about safety and care. Good lighting, open shops, and people on the streets help everyone feel safe. A city that works at night must look after those who keep it going. As dawn nears, the city begins to wake once more. Milkmen, newspaper sellers, and early workers appear on the roads. The night fades and the daily rhythm starts again. A city at night is never truly asleep, it only changes its work and its mood. Those who walk its streets after dark see a quiet beauty that day crowds hide. Night gives the city time to breathe before the next busy day begins. Each night shows a side of the city that daylight hides.",B.d6)
B.Uv=new A.eY("Choosing a Career","Deciding what to do for a living is rarely a single moment of clarity. Most people try a few different paths, discover what they enjoy and what drains them, and slowly adjust their direction. Patience with this process matters more than getting it right immediately. A career choice feels heavy because it touches money, identity, and daily life all at once. Young people often feel pressure to choose quickly and choose well. Parents, teachers, and friends all offer advice with good intent. Yet no one else can live the working days that follow the choice. The decision belongs to the person who will do the work. A useful first step is honest self knowledge. Notice which tasks give energy and which tasks take it away. Notice which subjects hold attention for hours without effort. Notice which problems feel worth solving even when they are hard. These small signals point toward work that may suit a person well. Skills matter as much as interest in a good career choice. Interest without skill can fade when real work begins. Skill without interest can pay well but feel empty over time. The best fit often lies where interest and skill meet a real need. Trying real work is the fastest way to learn what fits. A short internship, a part time job, or a volunteer role teaches more than months of thinking. Real work shows the daily routine, the people, and the pace. It also shows whether the field rewards the strengths a person has. Talking to people already in the field helps a great deal. Ask them what a normal week looks like and what they wish they had known. Ask what is hard, what is boring, and what still brings joy. Honest answers from workers are worth more than glossy brochures. Money matters, but it should not be the only measure. A high salary with long unhappy hours can cost health and peace. A modest salary with growth, respect, and balance can build a good life. Think about the life the job supports, not only the pay it offers. Career paths are rarely straight lines from start to finish. Many successful people changed direction more than once. Each change carried skills and lessons into the next role. A first job is a starting point, not a life sentence. Learning never stops in a changing world of work. New tools and new fields appear every year. The ability to learn, adapt, and work with others stays valuable in every field. Build those core abilities and doors will open in many places. Setbacks are part of every career story. A rejected application or a failed project is painful but instructive. It shows gaps to fill and directions to adjust. Resilience grows each time a person recovers and tries again. Choose with care, try with courage, and adjust with patience. A mentor can also guide a young worker through early doubts and hard choices. Good mentors share honest stories about their own mistakes and what they learned. With time, effort, and honest reflection, the right path becomes clear. A good career is built over years, one honest step at a time.",B.d6)
B.dn2=s([B.Up,B.Uu,B.Uv],t.LJ)
B.Z_=new A.aP(B.R,B.a9,1,"F and J: Anchor Keys","Place your index fingers on F (\u0641) and J (\u062c) \u2014 the keys with bumps.","\u0641\u0641 \u062c\u062c\u062c \u062c\u0641\u062c \u0641\u0641\u0641 \u062c\u0641\u0641 \u0641\u0641 \u0641\u0641 \u0641\u0641\u0641 \u062c\u0641\u062c \u0641\u0641\u0641 \u0641\u0641 \u062c\u062c\u0641 \u062c\u062c\u062c \u0641\u062c\u0641 \u062c\u0641\u062c \u0641\u0641\u062c \u0641\u062c \u0641\u062c\u0641 \u062c\u062c\u062c \u062c\u0641\u062c \u0641\u062c\u0641 \u0641\u0641\u0641 \u0641\u0641\u0641 \u062c\u062c\u062c \u0641\u0641\u0641 \u0641\u0641\u0641 \u062c\u062c\u062c \u062c\u062c \u062c\u062c\u062c \u0641\u0641\u0641",90,0,"Stage 1 \xb7 Home Row","\u0641\u062c","Left index: F key (\u0641) \xb7 Right index: J key (\u062c)","Feel the bumps on F and J and rest your index fingers there; thumbs on the space bar. This is a phonetic layout: the letter you get matches the sound of the English key, so finger positions are the same as English. Sit straight, keep your wrists relaxed and look at the screen, not the keyboard.")
B.Z2=new A.aP(B.R,B.a9,2,"D and K: Middle Fingers","Add your middle fingers: D (\u062f) and K (\u06a9).","\u062f\u0641 \u062f\u062f\u062f \u0641\u062f \u062c\u06a9\u062c \u06a9\u0641 \u062c\u06a9 \u062f\u062f\u062f \u062f\u062f \u062f\u0641\u062f \u06a9\u062c \u062c\u06a9\u06a9 \u062f\u06a9\u062f \u06a9\u062c\u06a9 \u06a9\u06a9\u062c \u062f\u062f\u062f \u06a9\u06a9\u06a9 \u06a9\u0641\u06a9 \u062c\u06a9\u06a9 \u062f\u062f \u062f\u062f\u0641 \u062f\u062f\u062f \u062c\u062f\u062f \u062f\u062c \u062f\u062f \u062f\u062f \u06a9\u062f \u0641\u062f \u062f\u062f \u06a9\u062f\u06a9 \u06a9\u06a9\u06a9 \u062c\u06a9 \u062f\u06a9",90,0,"Stage 1 \xb7 Home Row","\u062f\u06a9","Left middle: D key (\u062f) \xb7 Right middle: K key (\u06a9)","Keep F and J anchored. Lift only the finger that types, then bring it back.")
B.Yv=new A.aP(B.R,B.a9,3,"S and L: Ring Fingers","Add your ring fingers: S (\u0633) and L (\u0644).","\u0633\u0633\u0633 \u0644\u0641\u0644 \u062c\u0644\u0644 \u0644\u0644\u06a9 \u062c\u0633\u062c \u0633\u0644 \u0644\u0633\u0644 \u06a9\u0644 \u0644\u0633 \u0633\u0633\u0633 \u062f\u0644 \u0644\u0644\u0644 \u0644\u0633\u0644 \u0641\u0633\u0633 \u0633\u0644 \u0633\u0644 \u0633\u0644 \u06a9\u0633 \u0641\u0633 \u0644\u0644 \u0644\u0633 \u0633\u0633\u0633 \u0644\u0641 \u062c\u0644 \u0644\u0644\u06a9 \u0633\u0633\u06a9 \u0644\u0633\u0644 \u0641\u0644 \u0644\u0644\u0644 \u0633\u062f\u0633 \u062f\u0644\u0644 \u0644\u0644\u0641",90,0,"Stage 1 \xb7 Home Row","\u0633\u0644","Left ring: S key (\u0633) \xb7 Right ring: L key (\u0644)","Ring fingers are weaker \u2014 go slowly and focus on accuracy.")
B.YH=new A.aP(B.R,B.a9,4,"A and ; : Little Fingers","Add your little fingers: A (\u0627) and semicolon key (\u061b).","\u0641\u0627\u0627 \u061b\u061b \u06a9\u0627\u06a9 \u062c\u061b\u062c \u061b\u061b \u061b\u061b\u0641 \u061b\u061b \u0644\u061b\u061b \u061b\u0633 \u061b\u0627\u061b \u0641\u061b\u0641 \u061b\u061b\u061b \u06a9\u061b \u061b\u061b \u0627\u0627 \u0627\u0627\u06a9 \u061b\u0644 \u0641\u0627 \u061b\u0627 \u061b\u061b\u061b \u061b\u0627 \u0627\u0627\u0627 \u0627\u0627 \u061b\u061b \u062f\u061b\u061b \u0627\u061b\u0627 \u0644\u061b \u0627\u061b\u0627 \u061b\u061b\u061b \u061b\u061b\u061b \u0627\u0641\u0627 \u061b\u0627\u061b",90,0,"Stage 1 \xb7 Home Row","\u0627\u061b","Left little: A key (\u0627) \xb7 Right little: ; key (\u061b)","Your home row is now complete: A S D F (left hand) and J K L ; (right hand).")
B.YA=new A.aP(B.R,B.a9,5,"G and H: Index Stretch","Stretch your index fingers to G (\u06af) and H (\u06be).","\u06be\u0627\u06be \u06af\u06af \u0641\u06be \u0627\u06af\u06af \u06be\u06af \u06af\u06be \u06be\u06be \u06af\u06af \u06af\u06be\u06af \u0641\u06af \u06a9\u06be \u06af\u06af \u06be\u06be\u06be \u06be\u062c \u06be\u06be \u06be\u06be \u06a9\u06be \u06a9\u06af \u0627\u06af\u06af \u06af\u06af\u06af \u06a9\u06be\u06a9 \u06be\u06be\u06be \u0627\u06be \u06a9\u06af\u06a9 \u06af\u06be\u06af \u06be\u06af\u06be \u062f\u06af\u06af \u062f\u06be\u06be \u06af\u06af \u06af\u062c\u06af \u06be\u06be\u0627 \u06af\u06be\u06af",90,0,"Stage 1 \xb7 Home Row","\u06af\u06be","Left index: G key (\u06af) \xb7 Right index: H key (\u06be)","Stretch sideways, then return the finger to F or J straight away.")
B.YE=new A.aP(B.R,B.a9,6,"Home Row Words","Real Urdu words made only from home row letters.","\u0633\u0627\u0644 \u062f\u0627\u0644 \u062c\u0627\u0644 \u06af\u0627\u0644 \u0641\u0627\u0644 \u0633\u0627\u0644\u06a9 \u062c\u0644\u062f \u06af\u0644\u0627\u0633 \u06a9\u0644\u0627\u0633 \u062c\u0644\u0627\u0644 \u062f\u06a9\u06be \u0633\u0627\u06af \u06af\u06be\u0627\u0633 \u06a9\u06be\u0627\u0644 \u06a9\u06be\u0644\u0627 \u062c\u06be\u0627\u06af \u0633\u06a9\u06be \u0644\u06af\u0627 \u062f\u0644 \u06af\u0644 \u06a9\u0644 \u062c\u0644 \u0633\u062c\u0627 \u0633\u0644\u0627\u062f \u0627\u06af\u0644\u0627 \u06a9\u06be\u0627 \u062c\u0627 \u06af\u0644\u0627 \u0633\u0627\u06a9\u06be \u062c\u0627\u06af \u062c\u0644\u0627 \u06a9\u06be\u0644 \u062f\u06a9\u06be\u0627 \u0633\u06a9\u06be\u0627 \u0644\u06af",90,0,"Stage 1 \xb7 Home Row","","All eight fingers on the home row","Keep all fingers resting on the home row and return to it after every key.")
B.Z8=new A.aP(B.R,B.a9,7,"Home Row Review","Short practice to lock in the home row.","\u06af\u0644\u0627\u0633 \u06af\u0644 \u06a9\u06be\u0644\u0627 \u06a9\u0644\u0627\u0633 \u0633\u06a9\u06be\u0627 \u062f\u06a9\u06be\u0627 \u06a9\u06be\u0627\u0644 \u062c\u0644\u062f \u062c\u0627\u06af \u0633\u0627\u0644 \u06af\u0644 \u062f\u0644 \u06af\u0644\u0627 \u06a9\u0644 \u06a9\u06be\u0627 \u0627\u06af\u0644\u0627 \u0633\u06a9\u06be \u062c\u0644\u0627\u0644 \u0633\u062c\u0627 \u0633\u0627\u06af \u062f\u06a9\u06be",90,0,"Stage 1 \xb7 Home Row","","All eight fingers","If you lose your place, find the bumps on F and J again.")
B.Zw=new A.aP(B.R,B.a9,8,"E and I: Reach Up","Reach up with your middle fingers to E (\u0639) and I (\u06cc).","\u0639\u0639 \u0639\u061b\u0639 \u06cc\u06cc\u06cc \u062c\u06cc\u062c \u061b\u0639\u061b \u06cc\u062c \u06cc\u0639\u06cc \u0641\u06cc\u06cc \u06cc\u06cc\u06cc \u0639\u06cc\u0639 \u0639\u0639\u0639 \u06cc\u06cc\u06cc \u0639\u0639\u0644 \u0639\u0639\u061b \u0641\u06cc \u06cc\u06cc\u06cc \u0639\u0639 \u0639\u0639\u0644 \u062c\u06cc\u062c \u0639\u0644\u06cc \u062c\u06cc \u06a9\u06cc \u0633\u06cc \u062f\u06cc \u0644\u06cc \u0633\u06cc\u06a9\u06be \u062f\u06cc\u06a9\u06be \u062c\u06cc\u0644 \u06a9\u06cc\u0633 \u0639\u062f\u0644 \u0639\u06a9\u0633 \u062f\u06cc\u0627 \u06a9\u06cc\u0627 \u06af\u06cc\u0633 \u06af\u06cc\u0644\u0627 \u0633\u06cc\u06a9\u06be\u0627 \u062f\u06cc\u06a9\u06be\u0627 \u06a9\u06be\u06cc\u0644 \u062c\u06be\u06cc\u0644 \u0639\u0644\u0627\u062c",90,0,"Stage 2 \xb7 Top Row","\u0639\u06cc","Left middle: E key (\u0639) \xb7 Right middle: I key (\u06cc)","Reach up from D and K, then return to the home row.")
B.Yc=new A.aP(B.R,B.a9,9,"R and U: Index Fingers Up","Reach up with your index fingers to R (\u0631) and U (\u0621).","\u0621\u0621\u062c \u0631\u0631 \u0621\u0631\u0621 \u062c\u0631 \u0633\u0631\u0633 \u0621\u0631 \u0631\u0631\u0631 \u0621\u0621 \u0621\u0631 \u0631\u0631\u0644 \u0631\u0631\u0631 \u0633\u0631\u0633 \u0631\u0631\u0631 \u0621\u06be\u0621 \u0621\u0621\u0621 \u0631\u0631\u0631 \u06be\u0621\u0621 \u0621\u0621 \u0631\u062f \u0631\u0631 \u0631\u0627\u06af \u0631\u0633 \u062f\u0631\u062f \u06af\u0631\u062f \u0633\u0631\u062f \u06a9\u0631\u0633\u06cc \u062f\u0631\u06cc\u0627 \u06a9\u0627\u0631 \u0631\u06cc\u0644 \u0631\u06a9\u06be \u0631\u06a9\u06be\u0627 \u0631\u0633\u06cc \u0631\u06cc\u0633 \u0641\u06a9\u0631 \u0639\u0631\u0633 \u062f\u0639\u0627 \u0633\u0627\u0631\u06cc \u0633\u0631\u062f\u06cc",90,0,"Stage 2 \xb7 Top Row","\u0631\u0621","Left index: R key (\u0631) \xb7 Right index: U key (\u0621)",u.v)
B.YP=new A.aP(B.R,B.a9,10,"T and Y: Index Stretch Up","Stretch your index fingers to T (\u062a) and Y (\u06d2).","\u06d2\u062a \u062a\u062a \u062a\u062a\u062c \u062a\u06a9\u062a \u06d2\u06d2\u06d2 \u06d2\u062a\u06d2 \u0644\u062a \u06d2\u06d2 \u06d2\u062a \u06d2\u06d2\u0633 \u0641\u062a\u062a \u0639\u062a\u0639 \u062a\u062a \u062a\u06d2\u062a \u062a\u062a\u062a \u06d2\u0644 \u06d2\u06d2\u06d2 \u062a\u06cc \u062a\u06d2 \u06d2\u06d2\u0644 \u062a\u0627\u0631 \u062a\u06cc\u0631 \u0633\u062a\u0627\u0631 \u06a9\u062a\u0627 \u062f\u0644\u06cc\u0644 \u06a9\u06cc\u0633\u06d2 \u062c\u06cc\u0633\u06d2 \u0627\u06cc\u0633\u06d2 \u0644\u06cc\u06d2 \u06a9\u06d2 \u0633\u06d2 \u062a\u06cc\u0631\u06d2 \u062a\u0631\u06a9\u06cc \u06a9\u0631\u062a\u06d2 \u0633\u06cc\u06a9\u06be\u062a\u06d2 \u062f\u06cc\u06a9\u06be\u062a\u06d2 \u062c\u0627\u062a\u06d2 \u0633\u062a\u0627\u0631\u06d2 \u0633\u0627\u062a\u06be \u0631\u0627\u062a \u062c\u0644\u062a\u06d2 \u06af\u0644\u06d2",90,0,"Stage 2 \xb7 Top Row","\u062a\u06d2","Left index: T key (\u062a) \xb7 Right index: Y key (\u06d2)","Use the same index finger that types R or U \u2014 just stretch one key further.")
